    "chunk_size_bytes": 1601,
    "chunk_text": "other parties have to appear before court and argue and convince judges that they have no jurisdiction. I mean, this a blatantly fundamental position which ought not to be entertained, but that does not seem to be the situation in this country anymore, that expressions in this House are being challenged. I do not want to go into the specific cases because then, we ourselves will be violating tradition, the principles of sub judice and all that. So, I do not want to do that, but it is a worrying factor. This is certainly a matter that must worry all of us as to the relationship between these organs of government.\n\nWhat did he say? He said the Seventeenth Amendment to the Constitution had established the Constitutional Council as part of the Executive. That is wrong. He said that at that time, he, the Hon. K.N. Choksy and Hon. Karu Jayasuriya drafted that law. That is also wrong. It was at the insistence of the Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna at that time, in 2001, that it was brought in. He was talking about something else; he was talking about the aborted Eighteenth and the Nineteenth Amendments, which were never passed in this House and which he, during his tenure as Prime Minister, brought to clip the wings of the Executive President at that time. He was reading from the Determinations on those, claiming that they were part of the Determination on the Seventeenth Amendment. He even said that \"the Hon. Sumanthiran appeared in the case for the Centre for Policy Alternatives\". I was bemused because in 2001, I was not in the country; I was reading for my Master\u2019s Degree elsewhere."
  },
