    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "f4e1b5d56ca28bcc9ab304a3bb4d4aac",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1758,
    "chunk_text": "Chapter created. \u201cThe Executive\u201d is Chapter VII and a new Chapter VIIA was created. Sui generis organization, the Constitutional Council is neither a part of the Executive nor a part of the Legislature. On that day, I made an intervention. I did not say it was part of the Legislature; I said it was part of the legislative structure and that is what the Determination on the Seventeenth Amendment also says, I later found out. It further states, I quote:\n\nSo, it states \"with the Speaker as its Chairman\". That is why it is part of the legislative structure. The Speaker is the Chairman and the body, always, has met in Parliament. But, that does not make it part of the Legislature, in the strict two senses of the word.\n\nIn the part which the Hon. President quoted, which is actually from the Determination on the Eighteenth Amendment to the Constitution and appears at page 306 of the same Volume, there is a statement that it is the Executive power. The reason for that is this. The Minister of Justice, the Hon. (Dr.) Wijeyadasa Rajapakshe, and I also appeared in this case on the Eighteenth Amendment to the Constitution Bill and I distinctly remember your submissions, Hon. Minister, in the Supreme Court then, which were primarily aimed at the language. I remember you saying, \u201cThis is bad English. How can this be part of our Constitution?\u201d, something which the President claims he and the Hon. Choksy drafted, hurriedly drafted, perhaps. It goes on to state, I quote:\n\n\u201cThe Constitutional Council established under the 17th Amendment, is part of the Executive and is attributed executive power.\u201d\n\nThat is for the reason that they were considering the rules which were to be made part of the Constitution for the Constitutional Council."
  },
