    "chunk_size_bytes": 1961,
    "chunk_text": "I actually want to draw your attention to the very critical situation we are facing in terms of the Judiciary, justice and democracy in this country, the role of the Judiciary at this moment and how important it is. I think we all agree that for a country or for a democratic society to function effectively, not only do the various organs of the State or the pillars of democracy need to function effectively, but they also need to be seen legitimate by the people. There should be a level of trust between the people and the various organs of the State. A social contract between the various organs of the State and the people is a necessary element in governing. There is an unwritten social contract between the various organs or institutions of the State and the people and some level of trust that that social contract would be respected and protected. In that sense, the trust the people have placed in the institutions of justice or the Judiciary is critical. There needs to be some trust that those institutions would function impartially, independently and that they would act in the interest of the people. The problem today is that the trust the people have in the organs of the State have deteriorated to a critical level. Today, the situation we have is actually a distortion of that social contract, a distortion of the trust that the people have and a manipulation of that trust.\n\nGovernment. Within two years, they completely managed to destroy the trust the people had placed in that Government. The bottom line was that the institutions that were expected to take care of the people and to protect the people essentially failed. Rather, those institutions were manipulated or made to function in the interest of those in power. This is what forced the people to take to the streets; this is what forced the people to demand that those elected Representatives leave their positions. But, what is more critical is that what happened thereafter."
