    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "295b9b0043bdf44aa106c24bb0af6462",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1999,
    "chunk_text": "[Interruption] Thank you, Hon. Minister. The Hon. Minister made commitments on that in 2015 when he went to Geneva with the late Hon. Mangala Samaraweera. It was during your tenure as Minister of Justice at that time that the Government of Sri Lanka made commitments that they would create a moratorium on the application of the PTA.\n\nThe next matter, Sir, is something that the Hon. Sithadthan also mentioned, but he did not mention the entire number. Under the PTA, despite the fact that the Government\u2019s official position is that they would not use the PTA, the police continue to bring charges under \u201cB\u201d Reports. As you know, Sir, the Government\u2019s position is that people are entitled to remember their dead; that is their official position. The President, the Hon. Minister and others also have stated it, the proviso being that they must not commemorate as the LTTE, that they must not project it as an LTTE function. As a result, people have tried to go and attend those various commemorations. The police, particularly those in the Eastern Province and not in the North, had arrested ten individuals on the basis of their taking part or in some way, organizing those commemorations, out of whom two individuals, Mr. K. Kumararaja and K. Janojan, father and son, are members of our own party. All the courts in Batticaloa, when the police approached them to get a ban on the commemorations, refused to give a ban. They said that commemorating the dead is a fundamental right enshrined under the Constitution and that people are entitled to do it, provided that there is no LTTE paraphernalia being projected, that the LTTE is not being projected during those events. So, there was no LTTE being projected; everybody went there to commemorate the dead. Despite that, when those two individuals went for that, they were turned back. The police refused to allow them to commemorate the dead at the cemeteries, where those former cadres were buried or cremated, and they had to turn back."
  },
