    "chunk_text": "You know that by 2015, there were over 350 detainees under the PTA and with the assistance of the Hon. Attorney General, we released a large number of detainees. Thereafter, to hear out cases of detainees with serious charges under the PTA and conclude them, we made arrangement to establish two separate High Courts, one in Colombo and one in Anuradhapura. In fact, within a period of one and a half years or two years, they could have finished those cases very easily because there were not more than 100 cases. I also checked the progress of those courts from time to time. On most days, what had happened was that the lawyers had come and asked for future dates.\n\nTherefore, during that period they could not conclude those cases, but with all that, now it has come down to less than 20 cases altogether of convicted and remand prisoners.\n\n\u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0dc0\u0dd3.\u0dc0\u0dd3. \u0daf\u0db4\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dca\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbd\u0db8\u0dca \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9c\u0bc0.\u0b9c\u0bc0. \u0ba4\u0baa\u0bbe\u0ba9\u0bcd\u0ba9\u0bae\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bb2\u0bae\u0bcd) (The Hon. G.G. Ponnambalam) No, nine convicted and five others.\n\n2015 2016\n\n\u0db4\u0daf\u0d9a\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0dca \u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\u0dca\u200d\n\n\u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0da2\u0db1\u0dcf\u0db0\u0dd2\u0db4 \u0d86 \u0dad\u0dd2 \u0d86\u0da5 \u0db1\u0d86\u0da0\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dba) \u0dc0\u0dd2\u0da2\u0dba\u0dd9\u0dcf\u0dc3 \u0dbb\u0dcf\u0da2\u0db4\u0d9a\u0dca\u0dc2\u0dca \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9a\u0ba9\u0bbe\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0baa\u0ba4\u0bbf \u0b9a\u0b9f\u0bcd\u0b9f\u0ba4\u0bcd\u0ba4\u0bb0\u0bc8\u0bbf (\u0b95\u0bb2\u0bbe\u0ba8\u0bbf\u0ba4\u0bbf) \u0bb5\u0bbf\u0b9c\u0baf\u0ba4\u0bbe\u0bb8 \u0bb0\u0bbe\u0b9c\u0baa\u0b95\u0bcd\u0bb7) (The Hon. (Dr.) Wijeyadasa Rajapakshe, PC) Sir, with regard to the other prisoners also - I think the Hon. Jayantha Weerasinghe explained it - we would take action under the sentencing policy we are going to bring in to Parliament. Some have been detained for longer periods than their sentences. A person's sentence commences from the date his sentence is confirmed either by the Court of Appeal or the Supreme Court. So, the legal system itself is faulty; that is wrong. Now, we are trying to correct it. I think had it been corrected earlier, a lot of people would have been released. I am hopeful that I would be able to bring in that Bill and get it passed very soon, maybe, in January or February next year."
