    "chunk_index": 154,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "f67fdb8e36c41c53fc73b1ff3b762625",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2007,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, I would also like to bring up another sensitive matter. That is, every 90 minutes, a woman is raped in Sri Lanka. These are not official statistics, but outcomes from surveys. Most women who are raped do not report it. But, we cannot use that as an excuse and say that women are not being raped.\n\nopinion, but, again, these are numbers based on surveys, reports and studies, which have always been available to us, but which we have not acted on.\n\nDespite these alarming statistics on rape and sexual violence, women are often blamed and punished for it. The criminalization of abortion is a major example for that. With that being said, I would also like to say that according to a 2016 estimate done by the Ministry of Health in Sri Lanka, approximately 658 abortions take place a day in this country. That is, close to 250,000 abortions per year and 250,000 Sri Lankan women whose lives are endangered or at risk.\n\nThen, if we take the impact on maternal health, a research from 2015 indicates that unsafe abortions are the third most common cause for perinatal deaths in Sri Lanka, accounting to 10 to 13 per cent of maternal fatalities. This highlights a serious health risk. With regard to legal restrictions, Sri Lanka has some of the most restrictive abortion laws in the world. Abortion is illegal unless it is necessary to save the life of the mother. Engaging in abortion is a criminal act under Sri Lanka's Penal Code and is punishable with imprisonment, ranging from three to ten years with or without a fine.\n\n\u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0db8\u0dd6\u0dbd\u0dcf\u0dc3\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dd6\u0daa \u0db8\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb\u0dd3\u0dd4\u0dbb\u0db8\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0ba4\u0b95\u0bb2\u0b95\u0bae\u0ba4\u0bbe\u0b99\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bcd \u0b89\u0bb1\u0bc1\u0baa\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bbf\u0ba9\u0bb0\u0bcd \u0b85\u0bb5\u0bb0\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bb3\u0bcd) (The Hon. Presiding Member) Hon. Minister, please wind up now. \u0d9c\u0dd4 \u0da2\u0dd3\u0dc0\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb \u0daf\u0dad\u0dca\u0dcf\u0dab\u0dca\u0da9\u0db8\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dca\u0dcf (\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0baa\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bc1 \u0b9c\u0bc0\u0bb5\u0ba9\u0bcd \u0ba4\u0ba4\u0bbe\u0ba3\u0bcd\u0b9f\u0bae\u0bbe\u0ba9\u0bcd) (The Hon. Jeevan Thondaman) I will, Sir."
  },
