    "chunk_text": "obviously, to proceed with that housing project, people struggle. So, what has happened over time, especially, in the case of those women-headed households, is that they are forced to take a loan to complete that first stage in order to get the second stage of funds to be released from the Government and by doing that, they get into a rut, they get into a vicious debt cycle.\n\nThen, the education system for the children in this country, Sir, very unfortunately, is not, if I may say so, geared towards the country\u2019s development, production and economic development. There is a fair criticism that we formulate our education policies on the basis of trying to get aid from the World Bank and from the Asian Development Bank.\n\nAlso, the criticism is that we, ultimately, produce people who have qualifications as opposed to the skills that are actually relevant for this country. I think the Hon. Members in the Government side and even those in the opposited side have, on several occasions, agreed with the sentiment that our education system has to be completely overhauled. We need to produce genuine entrepreneurship, people with an investment mind, but none of which, unfortunately, happens. It is only an elite few who have access to private and foreign education institutions who eventually succeed very well in this country. It is not that we do not have the talent, but it just means that we are not providing the system with adequate capacity to produce those kinds of qualified people.\n\nSir, our schooling structure has cadres for counselling. If you take the North and the East, counselling is a crucial part because we have a drug problem that is rampant. If I may say with utmost responsibility, the military is involved in it. As you know, we have still not got out of"
