    "chunk_size_bytes": 1799,
    "chunk_text": "Another reason why this brain drain is taking place is, the way in which taxation is handled. University teachers have been affected by the new tax regime. The Federation of University Teachers Association - FUTA - has submitted some reforms to the current tax regime. Amendments are needed to that, Hon. State Minister. The Government has to seriously take into consideration an individual's ability to pay taxes. A blanket system that applies uniformly to everyone is unfair. Factors such as the number of dependents of the individual, the previous loans he has obtained et cetera should be taken into consideration in that. The introduction of a wealth tax and the taxation of big businesses, I think, should be the way out, not so much the current approach. So, I think that is another reason which is causing this brain drain.\n\noffered here. So, unless we get our house in order, we are going to lose in this matter.\n\nAnd also, Hon. Minister, the probationary lecturers have to sign a bond, if they want to go on study leave to pursue postgraduate programmes overseas. That amount of the bond is huge and that is mainly because the Government is factoring the scholarship money provided by western universities and scholarship-warding institutions such as the Fulbright Commission in calculating the bond amount. Due to the depreciation of the rupee, that figure has risen exponentially. Probationary lecturers are unable to find guarantors. Young academics with a commitment to serve the country are sadly resigning from their positions. This also discourages young academics from pursuing their higher studies in internationally acclaimed universities overseas. Therefore, the system which factors in scholarship money and other stipends provided by overseas institutions should be abolished."
  },
