    "md5": "1f2de59c1e962173551734f0d416132f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1666,
    "chunk_text": "Further, deficiency in furniture affects both students and teachers in a majority of schools. These schools continue to rely on furniture provided more than 10 to 15\n\nyears ago, which need reparation, but no funds are allocated for the same.\n\nPrincipals and teachers have been grappling with salary irregularities since 1997. As you are aware, as a result of implementing Public Administration Circular No. 2/97 issued based on the Report of the B. C. Perera Salary Commission, a salary anomaly was created for principals and teachers. While the Hon. (Dr.) Susil Premajayantha has approved a policy to overhaul the salaries of retired principals and teachers, there has been no implementation of it by the relevant Ministry, nor do we see any funds allocated in the Budget for that purpose. It is unfortunate that teachers and principals are compelled to either resort to legal action or to take to the streets to advocate for their rights. The situation becomes even more disheartening when they face mistreatment and brutality from the police or security officers during such protests. I respectfully implore the Hon. Minister to promptly address that issue of salary anomaly affecting the principals and teachers.\n\nThe English Diploma holders who have successfully completed their HNDE courses and applied for the available positions faced the open competitive examination held on the 27th of February, 2021 for recruitment as English teachers for Grade 3-I (C) in Eastern Province. Subsequently, they were selected following the interviews on the 25th and 26th of July, 2023, but are experiencing delays in their appointments due to non-approval by the Treasury."
